>=20
> I had a problem asking for the translations of political parties
> because those words mean something different than what they originally
> did and what they did 100, 50, and even 10 years ago.=A0 The words
> themselves show this in their construction.=A0=20

We are lojbanists, not interpreters of history.=A0 That means we must,=20
in effect, 'translate' the names of the parties even if we believe the name=
s=20
do not reflect today's reality.=A0 For example, the most often-used lujvo=
=20
for communism has been {guntrusi'o} based on the idea (vaguely) of having=
=20
communes ruled by workers (and peasants).=A0 Few people today would=20
claim that that ever was the case.=A0 But you would never get agreement=20
on what communism really represented.=A0 Communism is an ideology=20
justifying:

1) rule-by-state-biurocrats?
2) rule-by-party-clique-using-the-trappings-of-ideological-communism?
3) rule-by-well-meaning-ideologues-with-no-control-over-party-functionaries=
?
4) rule-by-workers-corrupted-by-power?
5) etc

The same would apply to socialsm, conservatives etc.

We are stuck with what the 'owners' of the label want it to mean.

mu'o mi'e .totus.

